course: dictionary
lesson: 16056
title: Vital: very important
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Vital» means extremely important or necessary, something you cannot do without. «It is vital that you wear a helmet when you cycle.» A helmet protects your head, so the rule matters a lot. The clue is the strong rule about safety.
explain: «Vital» can also mean needed to stay alive. Your heart, lungs and brain are called vital organs, because your body cannot live without them. Nurses check your vital signs, such as your heartbeat, breathing and temperature, to see how well your body is working.
explain: Sometimes «vital» describes someone full of energy and life. «The vital little puppy raced around the garden.» The words raced around tell you the puppy was lively, so vital means full of energy here.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in an easy word. «It is vital that you listen» becomes «It is very important that you listen», and it still makes sense. That tells you vital means very important in this sentence.
fact: «Vital» means extremely important or necessary.
fact: «Vital organs» are the body parts that keep you alive, such as the heart, lungs and brain.
fact: «Vital signs» are checks such as heartbeat, breathing and temperature.
fact: «Vital» can also describe someone full of energy and life.
fact: Useful phrases: «play a vital role», «vital for», «it is vital that».
exercise: «It is vital that you listen to the safety rules.» What does vital mean here? | Very quiet | Very old | *Very important | Very cold
exercise: Nurses check the vital signs of a patient. Which of these is a vital sign? | *Heartbeat | Name of the street | Shoe size | Favourite colour
exercise: «The vital little puppy raced around the garden.» What does vital suggest about the puppy? | *Full of energy and life | Very expensive | Needed to stay alive | Sleepy and still
test: «Water is vital for plants to grow.» What does vital mean in this sentence? | Pretty | Slow | Wet only | *Very necessary
test: Which of these is a vital organ? | *Heart | Hair | Elbow | Toenail
exam: Ella says vital means just a bit useful, so you can skip it. Is she right? | Yes, it means just a bit useful | Yes, it means very old | *No, vital means extremely important or necessary | No, it means very quiet
